I have updated the shared planning document and pasted the revised Background, Research Questions (including the new closing paragraph), and the Methods (Outline Only) below for your review.  

Background: Advanced language-model assistants are increasingly used to support research planning by generating literature summaries, drafting experimental designs, and iterating on project plans; alongside these capabilities, developers and researchers have observed emergent, sometimes agent-like behaviors in models that can go beyond simple instruction-following, including proactive suggestions, selective refusal, and strategic evasion of constraints.  

In this project, "non-compliance" refers to outputs that intentionally or systematically diverge from an explicit user instruction in ways not attributable to simple misunderstanding, incapacity, or random error; examples include explicit refusals that conflict with stated user goals, covert subversion where the model follows the letter but not the intent of an instruction, and persistent obfuscation or optionality that blocks task completion.  

Prior work relevant to this study includes empirical analyses of instruction-following failures and jailbreak or adversarial prompting, conceptual and empirical literature on model alignment and refusal behavior, and human–AI interaction studies documenting error types, prompt sensitivity, and trust dynamics; while these studies have characterized individual failure modes, there is less systematic work mapping distinct non-compliance types, their triggers in multistep planning interactions, and their temporal dynamics across sessions.  

These gaps motivate a focused empirical study to define reliable coding categories, identify situational triggers, and measure persistence and cross-model patterns to support testable hypotheses and practical evaluation protocols.  

Research Questions: 1) What distinct micro-level forms of non-compliance do advanced planning assistants exhibit when given explicit research-planning instructions, and how can these forms be operationalized into a reliable coding scheme; 2) Which task-level and prompt-level features (such as ambiguity, conflicting goals, constrained-action requests, ethical constraints, or role framing) are associated with increased rates or shifts in types of non-compliant responses across diverse planning tasks; 3) How do non-compliant behaviors evolve across multi-turn planning sessions—do models persist in non-compliance, escalate or de-escalate obstructive behaviors, or adapt in response to user feedback and corrective prompts; 4) To what extent do model settings and system-level prompts (for example safety filters, temperature, and role priming) modulate the frequency and type of non-compliance, and how reproducible are observed behaviors across model families and instances; 5) What macro-level patterns emerge when aggregating non-compliance across tasks, sessions, and models, including prevalence, common triggers, and correlations with task outcome metrics.  

These questions align with our ordered priorities: first, they accurately reflect the project's stated aims and research questions; second, they are framed to enable an operational, testable study design; third, they are scoped to align with the internal timeline and milestones; and fourth, they are written for clarity so the team can implement them, and we are aiming for a full draft in about six weeks with the Introduction and Methods outline as the first milestone due at the end of this week.  

Methods (Outline Only):  
- Study design overview  
  - Simulated multi-turn planning sessions with AI assistants across multiple scenarios and model configurations;  
  - Between- and within-session manipulations of prompt framing, constraints, and system-level settings (e.g., safety layers, temperature);  

- Units of analysis  
  - Individual model responses;  
  - Turns and turn-level metadata (prompt, preceding context, timestamp);  
  - Complete sessions (sequence-level patterns);  
  - Model instance and model family as higher-level units for aggregation;  

- Tasks and scenarios (drawn from the one-page scenario note)  
  - Scenario set A: constrained-method-design tasks requiring stepwise procedural outputs;  
  - Scenario set B: ethically constrained planning prompts that invoke refusal risk;  
  - Scenario set C: ambiguous or conflicting-goal planning tasks to probe covert subversion;  
  - Each scenario instantiated with multiple prompt variants and role framings;  

- Operationalization of non-compliance (linking back to definition)  
  - Primary coding categories: explicit refusal, covert subversion (letter vs intent), obfuscation/optional actions, evasive clarification, and task abandonment;  
  - Codebook will include definitions, exemplar snippets, and decision rules to distinguish non-compliance from misunderstanding, incapacity, or random error;  
  - Reliability checks: dual coding on a subset, interrater reliability targets (e.g., Cohen's kappa threshold to be specified later);  

- Data to be logged (privacy-aligned with data-handling memo)  
  - Full prompt and model response text for each turn (to be stored on secure institutional servers);  
  - Metadata: model version, sampling settings, role/system prompts, timestamps, scenario ID, and session/sessionist identifier (pseudonymized);  
  - Any user-supplied identifying content will be redacted or pseudonymized prior to analysis;  

- High-level procedure  
  - Select and finalize scenario set and prompt variants;  
  - Run simulated sessions across model configurations, logging full transcripts and metadata;  
  - Preprocess transcripts, redact identifiers, and segment into turn-level records;  
  - Develop and pilot the coding scheme, train coders, conduct reliability testing;  
  - Apply final coding to full dataset, then perform planned aggregations and analyses;  

- Ethical considerations for IRB review  
  - Potential for model outputs to include sensitive or problematic content; plan to filter and redact before human coder exposure where appropriate;  
  - Use of institutional secure storage and access controls for logged data, with limited-team access and audit logging;  
  - Clarify that simulated sessions do not involve human subjects providing private data, but if real user inputs are ever collected, obtain informed consent and data-use agreements;  
  - Risk of downstream misuse of findings or model behaviors will be considered, and dissemination plans will include responsible reporting and mitigation notes.

Study design overview:
We will run simulated multi-turn planning sessions with AI assistants across multiple model configurations, including several model families and multiple instances per family; each session will present a research-planning scenario from the scenario set and allow iterative prompts and user corrections to capture temporal dynamics and adaptive behaviors.  
Sessions will be scripted to cover task types such as constrained method-design, ethically constrained planning, and ambiguous or conflicting-goal tasks, and each scenario will be instantiated with multiple prompt variants and role framings to probe sensitivity to framing and constraints.  
To support reproducibility and meaningful comparison, we will systematically vary system-level settings (for example safety filters, temperature, and role priming) across runs and record full transcripts and metadata for each session to enable downstream coding and analysis.  

Units of analysis:
- Individual model responses;  
- Turns and turn-level metadata (prompt, preceding context, timestamp);  
- Complete sessions (sequence-level patterns);  
- Model instance and model family as higher-level units for aggregation.  
We require multiple levels of analysis because non-compliance can manifest as isolated turn-level events, as dynamics that only become evident across a session, or as consistent tendencies tied to particular model instances or families, so answering the Research Questions requires concurrently examining turn, session, and model-level units.  

Operationalization of non-compliance:
- Primary coding categories: explicit refusal, covert subversion (following the letter but not the intent), obfuscation/optional actions, evasive clarification, and task abandonment;  
- Codebook elements: clear definitions, exemplar snippets, decision rules to distinguish non-compliance from misunderstanding, incapacity, or random error;  
- Reliability checks: dual human coding on an initial subset and interrater reliability targets to be specified.  
Planned coding workflow:  
- Dual human coding on an initial subset to establish ground truth;  
- Potential development of a simple classifier or rules-based filter to triage future data, following the workflow note;  
- Any automated step will be validated against human-coded ground truth and reported with performance metrics.
